{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":504071,"num_turns":28,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"4192d174-4f06-4c91-b277-a6d2aac2fa9d","total_cost_usd":2.3137,"usage":{"input_tokens":275512,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":37332,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":275955,"outputTokens":37357,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.3137,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**16/16 workloads pass**, 3.4–6.1x faster than the reference. `kernel.py` and `solution.json` are on disk.\n\n## What the kernel does\n\nOne fused Triton kernel replaces the reference's six separate passes over the data. The reference materializes `q_variance`, `query_norm`, then a rescaled `query_norm` — each a full round-trip to HBM. Mine loads each row once, computes the RMS, scales, applies the weight, and stores once. The `grid.y` dimension handles Q and K, so both tensors go through one launch.\n\n## Numerics\n\nI matched the reference's operation order rather than just its algebra. Two details mattered:\n\n- `tl.math.rsqrt` vs. `1.0/tl.sqrt` — algebraically identical, but they round differently. The reciprocal form dropped exact-match from 80.9% to 70.0% of elements and cut the tolerance margin measurably. I verified this directly and kept `rsqrt`, which is what `torch.rsqrt` maps to.\n- I confirmed on-device that `mean(-1)` and `sum(-1) * (1/D)` produce bit-identical results here, so computing the cheaper form is safe rather than assumed.\n\n## Performance\n\nThe reference point that shaped the work: a **pure copy kernel** on this GPU peaks at 6.0–7.0 TB/s, not the 8 TB/s spec. My kernel hits 6.12 TB/s on the largest workload — essentially the achievable memory ceiling. I swept ROWS × num_warps × num_stages and three structurally different kernel layouts (per-head, fused-QK-per-program, whole-token-tile); all plateaued at the same place, which confirms the bound is memory, not the kernel shape.\n\nThat left launch overhead, which dominated half the workloads. Triton's Python dispatch costs ~14µs for this signature. Caching the compiled kernel and calling its launcher directly cut the small-shape floor from ~22µs to ~10µs. Worth flagging: **the harness numbers did not move**, because it amortizes host overhead and times GPU work only. The optimization is real for end-to-end use but doesn't show up in the score.\n\n## Correctness beyond the harness\n\nThe direct-launcher path bypasses Triton's own specialization logic, so a stale cache entry would silently launch a kernel compiled under wrong assumptions — a bug that produces plausible wrong numbers rather than an error. I keyed the cache on every property Triton specializes over and tracked pointer alignment **per-argument** rather than OR-reducing it, since a combined flag can alias two different alignment patterns onto one entry.\n\nI then tested the failure modes the 16 workloads don't cover: unaligned storage offsets (1–4 elements), non-contiguous inputs and weights, side CUDA streams, varying eps including 0.0, and shape-interleaved repeats to exercise cache reuse. All pass. I also fetch the stream per-call via `_cuda_getCurrentRawStream` (0.06µs) instead of caching it, so a side stream is never wrong.","ttft_ms":3745,"ttft_stream_ms":3744,"time_to_request_ms":62,"type":"result","duration_ms":569241,"uuid":"1f996112-d5b5-40b6-ae41-45c0db9ec59e"}
